## ABSTRACT
Replacement of enzymes with nanomaterials such as atomically dispersed metal catalysts is one of the most crucial steps in addressing the challenges in biocatalysis. Despite the breakthroughs of single-atom catalysts in enzyme-mimicking, a fundamental investigation on the development of an instructional strategy is still required for mimicking biatomic/multiatomic active sites in natural enzymes and constructing synergistically enhanced metal atom active sites. Herein, \(\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{N C}\) catalysts with atomically dispersed Fe-Fe dual-sites supported by the metal-organic frameworks-derived nitrogen-doped carbon are employed as biomimetic catalysts to perform proof-of-concept investigation. The effect of Fe atom number toward typical oxidase (cytochrome C oxidase, NADH oxidase, and ascorbic acid oxidase) and peroxidase (NADH peroxidase and ascorbic acid peroxidase) activities is systematically evaluated by experimental and theoretical investigations. A peroxo-like \(\mathsf{O}_{2}\) adsorption in \(\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{N C}\) nanozymes could accelerate the \(\mathsf{O}{-}\mathsf{O}\) activation and thus achieve the enhanced enzyme-like activities. This work achieves the vivid simulation of the enzyme active sites and provides the theoretical basis for the design of high-performance nanozymes. As a concept application, a colorimetric biosensor for the detection of \(\mathsf{S}^{2-}\) in tap water is established based on the inhibition of enzyme-like activity of \(\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{N C}\) nanozymes.
## KEYWORDS
nanozymes, atomically dispersed dual-metal sites, oxidase-like activities, single-atom catalysis, sensors
## 1 Introduction
Nanozymes, the nanomaterials with enzyme-like catalytic activity, have caused great attention in the field of biosensing, biomedicines, and environmental science due to their splendid advantages in easy preparation, low cost, high stability, and tunable catalytic performance [1–5]. Since the first attempt of magnetic \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) nanoparticles with intrinsic peroxidase-like activities has been developed [6], various nanomaterials, such as noble metal nanostructures, carbon nanomaterials, and transition metal-based nanomaterials, have been discovered to exhibit enzyme-like properties [7–12]. However, conventional nanozymes always possess diversified size and morphology, complex composition and structures, resulting in some inevitable difficulties in the precise recognition of active sites and further understanding of the catalytic mechanism at the atomic scale.
Recently, due to the unique geometric/electronic structure, high metal atomic utilization efficiency, and the strong interaction between metal and support, single-atom catalysts (SACs) exhibit exceptional catalytic activity [13–22]. Among them, SACs
achieve the \(4\mathrm{e}^{-}/4\mathrm{H}^{+}\)reduction of \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) (Fig. S3 in the ESM) [32, 33]. Also, polyphenol oxidases could catalyze the oxidation of monophenols in the presence of \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) molecules, where the process of \(\mathrm{O}-\mathrm{O}\) cleavage in polyphenol oxidases occurs at the double copper sites, similar to \(\mathrm{CcO}\) [34, 35]. Significantly, atomically dispersed dual-metal atoms have demonstrated superior catalytic activity toward \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) reduction in comparison to SACs, where atomic pairs with favorable electronic and geometric structures metal dimers are beneficial for the adsorption/desorption of the reaction intermediate and the cleavage of the \(\mathrm{O}-\mathrm{O}\) bonds [36–40]. The binuclear biocatalysis involved in natural systems and the exceptional catalytic activity of dual-atom catalysts inspire us to further investigate the enzyme-like activities of dual-atom catalysts, achieving the ultimate goal of vivid mimicking of natural enzymes.
Herein, to vividly mimic the binuclear active sites of natural enzymes, we employed the \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{NC}\) nanozymes with Fe-Fe dimer as a coordination center on zeolitic imidazolate framework (ZIF-8)-derived nitrogen-doped porous carbon \(\mathrm{(Fe_{2}N C)}\) as dual-atom nanozymes (Fig. 1). Both experimental and theoretical investigations revealed that \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{NC}\) nanozymes exhibit higher oxidase and peroxidase-like activities than \(\mathrm{Fe_{1}N C}\) (single-atom Fe on nitrogen-doped porous carbon). Owing to the unique peroxo-like \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) adsorption configuration, \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{NC}\) nanozymes could lengthen the distance of \(\mathrm{O}-\mathrm{O}\) bonding, accelerating the \(\mathrm{O}-\mathrm{O}\) cleavage and thus achieving the improvement of enzyme-like activities, which provide guidance in the design of high-efficient nanozymes. Finally, a colorimetric biosensor was established for the detection of \(S^{2-}\) in tap water based on the inhibition of Fe–N active sites.
## 2 Results and discussion
To accurately design the \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{NC}\) nanozymes, the \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{CO})_{9}\) compounds with well-defined \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\) dimers as active centers were in situ encapsulated in zeolitic imidazolate framework (ZIF-8) to form \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{CO})_{9}@\mathrm{ZIF}-8\) complex. After that, the \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{CO})_{9}@\mathrm{ZIF}-8\) complex was calcined at \(800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) under the argon atmosphere. Accordingly, ZIF-8 was carbonized to form porous nitrogen-doped carbon, and \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{CO})_{9}\) in the cage of ZIF-8 was transformed into \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\) dimers anchored by nitrogen on the porous carbon, resulting in the formation of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{NC}\) nanozymes with atomically dispersed \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\) dimers [41]. Similarly, the \(\mathrm{Fe_{1}N C}\) was also prepared by using iron acetylacetonate as precursors. All these samples possess dodecahedron features and are similar to ZIF-8-derived porous carbon (Figs. 2(a) and 2(d), and Fig. S4 in the ESM). Furthermore, the \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\) dimers interspersed in the support are directly observed from the image of the aberration-corrected high-angle annular dark-field scanning transmission electron microscopy (AC-HADDF-STEM, Fig. 2(e)). Similarly, single-atom Fe could be observed in their
supports (Fig. 2(b)). Previously, the local structural information of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{NC}\) has been systematically investigated by the X-ray absorption fine structure spectra and corresponding theoretical calculations [41]. \(\mathrm{Fe_{1}N C}\) had the single site Fe with four coordinated nitrogen atoms as active sites. The coordination numbers Fe-Fe bonding in \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{NC}\) are 1.2. The active sites of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{NC}\) are atomically dispersed \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{N}_{6}\) species, and each Fe atom is bonded with three nitrogen atoms and one Fe atom. The fine structure information of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{NC}\) is displayed in Figs. 2(c) and 2(f). Accordingly, these nanozymes with well-defined active sites are the ideal models to investigate their enzyme-like activities to fill the gap between the nanoparticles and SACs.
In nature, oxidase can accelerate the \(\mathrm{O}-\mathrm{O}\) cleavage and achieve the reduction of \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\). We comprehensively investigated the \(\mathrm{CcO}\), NADH oxidase (NOX), and ascorbic acid oxidase (AAOX) activities of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{NC}\) \((x=1,2)\) nanozymes, respectively, to evaluate their \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) activation ability through UV–vis spectroscopy. Natural \(\mathrm{CcO}\) can catalyze \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) to oxidize ferrous cytochrome C (Cyt c) to produce ferric Cyt c through a direct \(4\mathrm{e}^{-}\)electron transfer process, resulting in the blue-shift of the absorption peak at around \(410\ \mathrm{nm}\) and the decline of signal intensity at around \(550\ \mathrm{nm}\) [42]. As displayed in Fig. 3(a), the experimental results demonstrate that \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{NC}\) nanozymes can oxidize ferric Cyt c while \(\mathrm{Fe_{1}N C}\) nanozymes have almost no \(\mathrm{CcO}\) activity, indicating that dual-site Fe atoms in \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{NC}\) nanozymes can efficiently activate \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) and accelerate the electron transfer. The NOX activities of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{NC}\) nanozymes were also investigated. In detail, NOX can oxidize NADH to \(\mathrm{NAD}^{+}\), resulting in the decline of a characteristic peak at around \(340\ \mathrm{nm}\). As shown in Fig 3(b), \(\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{NC}\) nanozymes exhibit higher NOX-like activities than \(\mathrm{Fe_{1}N C}\) nanozymes, consisting well with the \(\mathrm{CcO}\)-like activity of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{NC}\) nanozymes. In nature, NOX can be divided into NOX (I) and NOX (II). NOX (I) can catalyze NADH to \(\mathrm{NAD}^{+}\)by \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) to produce \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) while NOX (II) can catalyze NADH to \(\mathrm{NAD}^{+}\)by \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) to produce \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) through a direct \(4\mathrm{e}^{-}\)process [43, 44]. We further investigated the intermediate products in enzyme-like catalytic reactions of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{NC}\) nanozymes. As displayed in Fig. S5(a) in the ESM, the additional \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) is detected by using horseradish peroxidase (HRP) and tetramethylbenzidine (TMB) as probes [45]. The generation of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) is not observed in NOX-like catalytic processes, demonstrating that \(\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{NC}\) nanozymes can mimic the activity of NOX (II).
We next investigated the AAOX-like activities of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{NC}\) nanozymes. Ascorbic acid (AA) could be oxidized by AAOX to produce dehydroascorbic acid in the presence of \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\), leading to the decline of signal intensity at around \(265\ \mathrm{nm}\) (Fig. 3(c),
pink line). It is found that \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{NC}\) nanozymes possess higher AAOX-like activities than \(\mathrm{Fe}_{1}\mathrm{NC}\) nanozymes, further confirming that the \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) molecules are more easily activated on dual-Fe sites in \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{NC}\) nanozymes (Fig. 3(c)). As displayed in Fig. 3(d), the additional \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) is detected by using HRP and TMB as probes. Like natural AAOX, almost no \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) is detected in the enzyme-like reaction of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{NC}\) nanozymes, indicating that \(\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{NC}\) nanozymes can direct catalyze AA and \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) to produce dehydroascorbic acid and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) through a direct \(4\mathrm{e}^{-}\)path. To quantitatively evaluate the AAOX-like activities of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{NC}\) nanozymes, we established a calibration curve based on the enzyme activity of the natural AAOX (Fig. S5(b) in the ESM). Accordingly, the enzyme activity of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{NC}\) nanozymes is \(18.9\ \mathrm{U/mg}\), which is 23.33 times higher than that of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{1}\mathrm{NC}\) nanozymes \((0.81\;\mathrm{U/mg})\). Although the enzyme activity of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{NC}\) nanozymes still lags far behind that of natural AAOX \((400\;\mathrm{U/mg})\), \(\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{NC}\) nanozymes exhibit higher tolerance at high temperatures and strong acid/basic conditions (Figs. 3(e) and 3(f)).
NADH peroxidases and AA peroxidases are the typical metalloproteases, which can accelerate the oxidation of NADH and AA in the presence of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\). As expected, \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{NC}\) nanozymes possess higher NADH peroxidases-like and AA peroxidases-like activities than \(\mathrm{Fe}_{1}\mathrm{NC}\) nanozymes, indicating that \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) molecules are more easily activated on dual-Fe sites in \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{NC}\) nanozymes (Figs. S6(a) and S6(b) in the ESM). Furthermore, the peroxidase-like activities of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{NC}\) nanozymes were quantitatively evaluated by the Michaelis-Menten kinetics in the colorimetric reactions. The \(V_{\mathrm{max}}\) of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{NC}\) nanozymes \((7.27\times10^{-5}\;\mathrm{M/s})\) towards \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) is about 4.17 times higher than that of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{1}\mathrm{NC}\) nanozymes (Fig. S6(c) in the ESM). As for TMB, the \(V_{\mathrm{max}}\) of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{NC}\) nanozymes \((6.61\times10^{-7}\;\mathrm{M/s})\) towards \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) is about 3.37 times higher than that of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{1}\mathrm{NC}\) nanozymes (Fig. S6(d) and Table S1 in the ESM). The excellent enzyme-like activities indicate that \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{NC}\) nanozymes are more easily activate \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) than \(\mathrm{Fe}_{1}\mathrm{NC}\) nanozymes, providing great opportunities to accurately tune metal active sites for boosting nanozyme activities.
The theoretical investigations were performed to reveal the catalytic nature of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{NC}\) nanozymes at the atomic scale. Previous work has confirmed that \(\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{NC}\) nanozymes have comparable Fe loading \((\mathrm{Fe}_{1}\mathrm{NC};\,0.35\;\mathrm{wt.}\%\) ; \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{NC};\,0.38\;\mathrm{wt.}\%)\) and specific surface area, demonstrating that the changes of enzyme-like
activities are closely associated with the coordination environments of active sites [41]. As expected, the structural information of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{NC}\) nanozymes had been testified by the X-ray absorption fine structure spectra and corresponding theoretical calculations, which are beneficial for the establishment of theoretical models. There are two \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) adsorption configurations including superoxo- and peroxo-like features are taken place on the surface of nanozymes. Previous works have proved that the \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) adsorption configuration at the single Fe sites at \(\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{NC}\) single-atom nanozymes is the superoxo-like structure [46]. Besides, some theoretical calculations have revealed that the peroxo-like \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) adsorption configuration at the dual Fe sites can accelerate the \(\mathrm{O}-\mathrm{O}\) bonding cleavage, which could be attributed to the fact that more electrons are donated into the empty orbitals of \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) toward better activation [41]. Based on the experimental results, almost no \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) was detected in the oxidase-like catalytic reactions. We concluded that the oxidation of Cyt c, NADH, and AA follows a four-electron transfer path. As shown in Fig. 4(a), we first established the \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) molecule adsorption model toward \(\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{NC}\) nanozymes. A superoxo-like adsorbed oxygen configuration was observed on \(\mathrm{Fe}_{1}\mathrm{NC}\) with an \(\mathrm{O}-\mathrm{O}\) bond length of \(1.25\,\mathrm{\AA}\). As a comparison, \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) on \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{NC}\) tends to form a peroxo-like configuration with a larger \(\mathrm{O}-\mathrm{O}\) bond length of \(1.45\,\mathrm{\AA}\), indicating that the peroxo-like configuration on \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{NC}\) with the significantly extended distance of \(\mathrm{O}-\mathrm{O}\) bond can accelerate the \(\mathrm{O}-\mathrm{O}\) cleavage. To explore the origin of the enhanced enzyme-like activity, the differential charge density was performed to reveal the electronic structure of the active site. As can be seen in Fig. 4(b), the local electrons around the \(\mathrm{Fe}_{1}\mathrm{NC}\) and \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{NC}\) active sites are all reduced, showing an oxidation state of Fe. The Bader charge of Fe \((q_{\mathrm{Fe}})\) was calculated to reveal charge changes at the active site quantitatively. The Bader charge of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{1}\mathrm{NC}\) is 1.07, which is higher than that of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{NC}\) \((q=0.81)\). Obviously, the oxidation state of Fe decreases as the number of Fe atoms increases, where \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{NC}\) shows the lowest oxidation state. Therefore, a lower oxidation state of Fe will result in a more effective activation of \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\). Furthermore, the project electronic density of states (PDOS) of oxygen adsorption state was calculated on the obtained \(\mathrm{Fe}_{x}\mathrm{NC}\) nanozymes (Fig. 4(c)). Meanwhile, the d-band centers of Fe and the p-band centers of O for each sample were also provided. The difference between the 3d band center of Fe and the 2p band center of O
in \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{NC}\) is smaller than that in \(\mathrm{Fe}_{1}\mathrm{NC}\), indicating that the 3d orbital of Fe and the 2p orbital of O overlap widely at \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{NC}\) active site, which is responsible for the strong adsorption of \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\).
on \(\mathrm{Fe}_{1}\mathrm{NC}\) \((-2.01\;\mathrm{eV})\), indicating the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) can be effectively activated at the \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{NC}\) active site.
Nanozymes as the efficient biosensing platforms have been widely investigated for the detection of small molecules (such as metal ions, glucose, and so on) [47, 48], cancer biomarkers [49], pathogens [50] and viruses [51], exhibiting satisfactory sensitivity and selectivity. In this work, as a concept application, we developed a colorimetric biosensor for the detection of \(S^{2-}\) in tap water based on the enzyme-like activities of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{NC}\) nanozymes. \(S^{2-}\) is the byproduct of periochemical, leather, and food processing industries, being extensively released into the environment and thus resulting in environmental pollution [52, 53]. Also, the abnormal level of the \(S^{2-}\) derivative \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) in vivo is closely associated with some diseases, such as Alzheimer's, Down's syndrome, and diabetes [54, 55]. Hence, sensitive and selective detection of \(S^{2-}\) plays a crucial role in the fields of environmental protection and human health. Herein, the proposed \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{NC}\) nanozymes with enzyme-like activities were used to sensitively detect \(S^{2-}\) in tap water. The detection principle is displayed in Fig. 5(a). TMB as the chromophoric molecules could be oxidized by \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}/\mathrm{O}_{2}\) with the help of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{NC}\) nanozymes as catalysts, generating a sharp blue peak at \(652\;\mathrm{nm}\). The \(S^{2-}\) could inhibit the enzyme-like activities of \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{NC}\) nanozymes, causing the color decrease in absorbance. Some experimental conditions, such as nanozymes concentrations and incubation times, were optimized to amplify the sensitivity (Fig. S10 in the ESM). As expected, following the increment of \(S^{2-}\) concentrations from \(1{-}800\;\upmu\mathrm{M}\), the absorbance values at
\( 652\,\mathrm{nm} \) of oxTMB are gradually decreased (Fig. 5(b)). Accordingly, the calibration curve of color change \( (A_{0}\mathrm{~-~}A/A_{0}) \), responding to the logarithmic \( S^{2-} \) concentrations is established (Fig 5(c)). The limit of detection for \( S^{2-} \) is calculated as to be \( 0.61~\upmu\mathrm{M} \). Also, the resultant colorimetric biosensor exhibits satisfactory selectivities in some anion and cation solutions (Fig. 5(d)). Furthermore, the standard addition recovery was used to evaluate the practical feasibility of this detection method. First, no \( S^{2-} \) is detected in the tap water. Then, the recoveries in Table S2 in the ESM are in the range of \( 94.21\%{-}100.31\% \), demonstrating that this colorimetric biosensor has the potential for practical application.
## 3 Conclusions
To sum up, we found that \( \mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{NC} \) nanozymes with atomically dispersed \( \mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{N}_{6} \) species as active sites could accelerate the O–O cleavage and thus achieve the enhanced oxidase-like and peroxidase-like activities. Theoretical investigations demonstrated that \( \mathrm{O}_{2} \) molecules are more likely to bind with \( \mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{NC} \) nanozymes through a peroxo-like \( \mathrm{O}_{2} \) adsorption configuration, which is similar to the natural heme-copper oxidase with the binuclear active sites. It is the first attempt that \( \mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{NC} \) dual-atom nanozymes exhibit higher enzyme-like activities than \( \mathrm{Fe}_{1}\mathrm{NC} \) single-atom nanozymes, which provides a theoretical basis for designing high-performance nanozymes. As a concept application, the \( \mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{NC} \) nanozymes were used to sensitively and selectively detect \( S^{2-} \) in tap water, exhibiting satisfactory feasibility in practical samples.
